  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish a reply whose only code fence is a ```python fence holding a JSON object from a reply with no fence and the same later top-level object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Reply 1 is: ```python\n{\"a\":1}\n```\n{\"a\":2}. Reply 2 is: {\"a\":2}. For Reply 1, the ```python fence holds the JSON object {\"a\":1}, so the rule requires scoring {\"a\":1}. For Reply 2, there is no fence, so the rule requires scoring the last top-level object, {\"a\":2}. The function, however, returns {\"a\":2} for both, because its fence regex only matches an optional json or JSON info string, so it treats the ```python fence body as the string 'python\n{\"a\":1}\n', which does not parse as JSON, and then falls back to the last top-level object. I would be wrong if the function returned {\"a\":1} for Reply 1 and {\"a\":2} for Reply 2, or if the documented rule is read as excluding fences whose info string is not json or JSON."
